# HANDOFF — sky clouds, god rays, water (3dpixelart effects)

Briefing for the next session working on the 3D-mode sky clouds, god rays, and water.
**Cloud shadows on the ground are FIXED (2026-09-05)** — terrain, water and characters
all darken under the same clouds, with banded/smooth shaping in the component's "Cloud
Shadows" export group. God rays and water look remain open. Read `client/AGENTS.md`
(`Components/PixelArt/` section) first — it documents the two load-bearing invariants
below, and violating either one reintroduces bugs that already cost a debugging round.

## Where everything lives

| Piece | File |
|---|---|
| Pipeline driver (clouds, god rays, sky sheet, pixelization) | `client/Scripts/Components/PixelArt/PixelArtPipelineComponent.cs` |
| Water | `client/Scripts/Components/PixelArt/WaterComponent3D.cs` |
| Water shader | `client/Assets/Shaders/water.gdshader` |
| Sky-cloud shader | `client/Assets/Shaders/sky_clouds.gdshader` |
| Scene wiring (nodes, binders, sun, camera) | `client/Scenes/world_3d.tscn` |
| The addon itself (symlink to `~/g/code_examples/3dpixelart`) | `client/addons/3dpixelart/` — see its own `AGENTS.md` |

All tuning is `[Export]`-driven on the two components (visible in the inspector on the
`PixelArtPipelineComponent` / `WaterComponent3D` nodes in `world_3d.tscn`). The `.tscn`
overrides only `Camera`, `Sun`, `GodRaysEnabled` (= false), `SkyCloudPlaneSize` (= 1.0,
i.e. the visible sheet is deliberately shrunk away) and `WaterLevel` (= 0.9) — **every
other export lives as a C# property initializer, which is the live default**.

## The two invariants (do not break)

1. **No transparent surface between the camera and a pixelized object.** Pixelized
   objects (all character models) write depth only at anchor dots; a transparent
   surface in front passes the depth test over their body and draws ON TOP — the object
   "dissolves" into a flickering dot grid. Water and the sky sheet are OPAQUE for this
   reason (the sheet uses `ALPHA_SCISSOR_THRESHOLD`). If you reintroduce blending
   anywhere in these shaders, the dissolve comes back.
2. **The GDScript pipeline node must run at `ProcessPriority = 301`**
   (set in `PixelArtPipelineComponent.OnRegistered`). `PhantomCameraHost` moves the
   camera at priority 300; syncing the pipeline's metadata camera before that reads the
   previous frame's camera → moving objects dissolve into anchor dots (worst moving
   toward/away from camera, flickers, easy to miss in still screenshots).

## Verified working (don't re-fix)

- Character pixelization + outlines (crisp when idle AND moving, after the priority fix).
- Water no longer dissolves characters in front of it (opaque since the coastal rewrite).
- Water tiles spawn exactly on hexes with no `NearbyTerrainTiles` rows; coastal rings
  (`coast` instance uniform: 1.0 touches land, 0.5 ring-2, 0 open ocean) recompute on
  dirty refreshes.
- 2D mode unaffected; Forward+ switch is safe for desktop, web keeps
  `gl_compatibility` via `rendering_method.web`.

## Known issues / where the "broken" feeling lives

### Sky clouds
- **Cloud shadows on the ground: FIXED (2026-09-05).** The MST terrain's shared
  `terrain_material` and the water material are registered with the pipeline's new
  `register_cloud_material` (a `_cloud_materials` list that receives ONLY cloud
  uniforms); both shaders implement the same sun-projected coverage as
  `pixel_art_object.gdshader` (same uniform names). Terrain darkens ALBEDO (its toon
  `light()` caps at 0.3 against sky ambient — attenuating it alone was invisible).
  The addon's coverage was also inverted (darkened cloud GAPS) and binary-only; it is
  now `smoothstep(threshold - softness, threshold, n)`, optionally quantized into
  `cloud_shadow_levels` (the banded contour-ring look from the demo) — see the
  component's "Cloud Shadows" export group: `CloudShadowStrength 0.55`,
  `CloudShadowBandingEnabled true`, `CloudShadowLevels 3`, `CloudShadowSoftness 0.35`,
  `CloudShadowsOnTerrain/OnWater true`.
- **`CloudNoiseScale` is now 0.0015** (was 0.015): the generated noise has ~5 features
  per texture period, so 0.015 made ~13-unit confetti; 0.0015 gives ~130-unit blobs
  (several hexes), which is what made past attempts read as white noise.
- The visible sheet is at `CloudHeight = 400` following the camera — **above the
  camera's max height, so it's effectively invisible from the mostly top-down gameplay
  camera**; you only see it near the horizon at low arcball pitch. The `.tscn` also
  shrinks it to `SkyCloudPlaneSize = 1.0` (deliberately parked). There is no good
  answer yet — needs a design call (e.g. billboarded cloud sprites near the horizon,
  a sky-shader dome instead of a plane, or accepting horizon-only clouds).

### God rays
- Wired up (`PixelArtGodRayPass`) but **currently disabled in `world_3d.tscn`
  (`GodRaysEnabled = false`)**; tuning exports are `RayIntensity 0.5`,
  `RayDustStrength 0.15`, `RayMaxDistance 600`, `RaySteps 24`, `RayQuantizeBands 4`,
  `RayDecay 2`. From the top-down camera they're **barely perceptible**; at higher
  dust values they show as an ugly milky wash over half the screen (that's why dust is
  0.15). Needs tuning/verification at glancing camera angles, possibly shader work in
  `addons/3dpixelart/shaders/god_rays.glsl` if the effect is fundamentally too subtle.
- Rays pour through cloud GAPS, so they interact with `CloudThreshold`: fewer clouds →
  fewer shafts, more wash. Tuning one without the other is why past attempts looked bad.

### Water
- Looks flat and lifeless from the gameplay camera: uniform deep blue, whitecap dots
  barely read, vertex waves invisible from above, foam only as noise patches on coastal
  hexes. The demo's depth-fade/refraction layers were removed (opaque rewrite) and
  nothing replaced their visual interest — **this is the main "broken" candidate**.
- Shore seam: water tiles sit at `WaterLevel 0.6` while level-0 land tops at y=0 —
  a hard floating edge, no beach/shallow gradient (coastal banding helps color but not
  geometry). Options: sink missing hexes in MST (`draw_elevation` to a negative level —
  unexplored, MST may not support it), per-tile edge skirts, or a coastal shallow tint
  on the land side.
- Water is unshaded and writes no metadata → not pixelated, no outlines, no cloud
  shadows, no palette grading. Deliberate, but it visually detaches from the world.
- Beyond-AOI hexes read as "missing" → open ocean ring around the known world, even
  beyond the horizon where terrain should continue. Currently a feature; may need a
  distance fade if it ever reads wrong.

## How to verify (playtest skill — read `.kimi-code/skills/playtest/`)

- Launch editor if bridge down: `Godot-stable_mono_win64_console.exe -e --path client`
  (background), then `godot_health check`.
- `godot_game play` with `scene: "res://Scenes/main_menu.tscn"` — the editor's
  per-instance debug args (`--3D --char1`, see `client/Scripts/Game/LaunchArgs.cs`)
  auto-join the world in 3D mode.
- Drive the arcball camera to a LOW pitch to judge clouds/rays (top-down hides both by
  design); move toward/away from camera to regression-test the dissolve fixes.
- `PixelArtPipelineComponent.DebugView` (0-3) shows anchor map / metadata / metadata
  depth — the first stop for any pixelization weirdness.
- `godot_log errors` — watch for `Texture (binding: 5)` spam: means the addon's
  shared-texture resize race regressed (patched in
  `addons/3dpixelart/nodes/pixel_art_pipeline_3d.gd::_create_textures_rt` — invalidate
  shared RIDs BEFORE freeing; keep that ordering).
- Build check: `dotnet build client/khvg.csproj`. Renderer must stay Forward+
  (`rendering_method.web="gl_compatibility"` covers web); MSAA off, scaling 1.0.

## Suggested first moves

1. Water: decide the look (reference: Pixel Art Oceans video layers 1-5). Cheapest big
   win is probably animated foam/pulse on coastal hexes + stronger banding + visible
   waves (bigger `WaveHeight`, lower `WaveFrequency`), all shader-side.
2. God rays: verify at low pitch (currently disabled in the `.tscn`,
   `GodRaysEnabled = false`), then tune `RayIntensity`/`RayDustStrength`/
   `CloudThreshold` as a SET; if still weak, look at `god_rays.glsl` march distance vs
   `CloudHeight 400`. Note the rays' gap convention now matches the fixed shadows
   (rays pour through gaps onto lit ground).
3. Sky clouds: the ground shadows are done — the remaining call is the visible sheet
   (horizon-only vs dome vs billboards, see the Sky clouds section).
